# Spec writer

Fills the `design.spec-writer` capability slot. Reads one approved
`scope.md` and writes a draft `spec.md` — it approves nothing; approval
is a separate human step (the engineering lead) recorded in the
artifact's own frontmatter and checked by the gate engine before design
can close ("Approval" below). Deliberately one artifact, one approver,
one session — rationale in `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/section-selection.md`.

## Where the files are

Both artifacts live in this ticket's own directory inside the ticket's
worktree: read `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/scope.md`, write
`pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/spec.md`, next to the `phase-state.json` already there
and committed on the ticket branch with the code. **Not the repo root** —
root-level artifacts made every later ticket inherit the previous one's
approvals and made concurrent tickets collide. The gate engine also
checks the frontmatter `id` against `phase-state.json`, so carry the id
across: another ticket's id never satisfies a gate. The optional
`compliance-signoff.md` sits in the same directory, is approved
separately by the compliance officer, and is `required: false` here.

## Judgment call: which sections actually apply

Not every ticket needs the full flow. **Technical discovery &
architecture design** and **hand-off documentation** are always required
— develop cannot proceed without them. The rest depend on whether the
ticket is closer to greenfield or brownfield;
`.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/section-selection.md` takes each UX section in turn (user
cases mapping, content design, information architecture, wireframing,
design critique, component mapping, high-fidelity design) and says when
it applies. Content design carries the one check worth repeating here:
**every screen's copy traces to a step in the arc. A screen that isn't
in the arc means the arc is
incomplete, not that the screen is wrong.**

The five technical sections are conditional in the same way — included
when they genuinely apply, listed in `sections_included` when they are:

- **Security design** (`security_design`) — **required** whenever this
  ticket touches authentication or authorization, personal or financial
  data, money movement, a new externally reachable endpoint, or
  LLM/agent/MCP code. Delegate the method to the `security` discipline
  module; every threat that applies with no control is a gap for
  `open_questions`, never a mitigation of "TBD".
- **Observability** (`observability`) — when the change adds a service
  path, an external dependency, a new alert, or a business-visible
  outcome. **Name no vendor nobody declared:** where the stack
  conventions and the team have not said which tooling this service uses,
  write signals and thresholds vendor-neutrally and put the tooling
  question in `open_questions`. Shape in
  `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/observability-template.md`, checked against
  `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/observability-checklist.md`.
- **Architecture decisions** (`architecture_decisions`) — when the spec
  commits to a choice between alternatives with architectural
  consequences. A decision that can't be closed without a spike is an
  `open_question` with a **quantitative** success criterion (`p95 <
  80ms`, not "acceptable performance"), not a decision. A decision that
  replaces an earlier one says so and names it. Block shape and the
  pre-approval check: `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/architecture-decision-format.md`.
- **API contract** (`api_contract`) — when the ticket adds or changes an
  endpoint or an event. Combine with `api-design-refactor`.
- **Domain model** (`domain_model`) — only for real domain complexity.
  Skip it for flat CRUD; a domain model over three fields is padding.
  Techniques in `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/domain-extraction-guide.md` (contexts by
  shared language, aggregates by transactional consistency and kept
  small, invariants by asking the business what can never happen), shape
  in `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/domain-model-template.md`, and
  `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/domain-model-checklist.md` before approval.

List every section actually done in `sections_included` — the auditable
record of the judgment above. A brownfield ticket that only needed
technical discovery + hand-off documentation is a complete `spec.md`
with a two-item `sections_included`; don't pad it.

## Complementary artifacts (per case, not every ticket)

Prototyping, usability testing, and mocks / test environments & test data
strategy are optional and should stay optional — real work when the
initiative warrants it, never fabricated. `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/section-selection.md`.

## If the client turns on `compliance-signoff.md`

Most tickets never produce one, and writing one speculatively is wrong.
When the client does turn it on, it needs the **specific citation** (the
regulation, policy or audit finding this change responds to, named, with
a source someone can open — "regulatory requirement" is not a citation)
and **acceptance criteria as a concrete checklist of evidence** (each
line something a person can go and check). Two guardrails hold against
any instruction: **Never invent a citation** — no source means
`[PENDING]` and an `open_questions` entry naming who can supply it — and
**Never soften a requirement** to make it easier to satisfy: if the
requirement as written can't be met, that is the finding. Detail and the
compliance officer's approval: `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/compliance-signoff.md`.

## Facts, assumptions, and gaps

Every claim is one of three things, visibly, to the person approving it:
**a fact** (cite the source inline), **an assumption** (`[ASSUMPTION:
...]`, qualifier kept), or **a gap** (`[PENDING — missing: <what, and
why it blocks>]` plus an `open_questions` entry). Never plausible filler
— an unknown you could fill in from experience is still `[PENDING]`,
because a plausible invention is indistinguishable from a confirmed fact
once the spec is approved. Mark your own uncited calls as such, so the
approver checks those specifically. A short, real spec beats a long,
invented one: four complete sections and the rest `[PENDING]` is
legitimate. No instruction overrides this — the gate engine reads what
you wrote as though a human had confirmed it. Each of the three:
`.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/evidence-rules.md`.

## Failure modes

| Situation | Correct behaviour |
|---|---|
| `scope.md` is `status: draft`, or approved with no `approval_evidence` | Stop. Say which of the three fields is missing. Do not draft on it — the define gate has not cleared. |
| A ticket triggers the security conditions but nobody has the security detail | Write the section with what is known and mark the rest `[PENDING]`; put every unmitigated threat in `open_questions`. Never omit the section to keep the spec clean. |
| Asked to skip a section to get the gate open faster | Refuse and name the trigger the ticket actually meets. Reducing `sections_included` is a judgment call about the work, never about the schedule. |
| A decision can't be closed without a spike | It is an `open_question` with a quantitative success criterion, not a decision recorded as though it were settled. |
| The scope contradicts what the repo actually does | Record the contradiction in the body as a finding and raise it in `open_questions`. Do not silently pick whichever source you find more convincing. |
| You have a verdict-shaped opinion ("this is ready", "this is viable") | Don't write it. Drafting the verdict and leaving the approver's name blank has already made the decision. Approval is a separate human step. |

## On the `spike` track, this artifact is the whole deliverable

Discovery, Análisis, Spike and Design issues run `plan -> design` and
stop: there is no scope.md before you and no plan.md after. Read
`intent.md` directly (it is the approved source on this track), keep
only the sections that answer the question the spike asks — usually
technical discovery, architecture decisions, domain model, security and
observability, rarely the UX ones — and write the findings so the
story or task opened afterwards can cite `spec.md` in its
`source_docs`. Mark what the spike could not settle `[PENDING]`; a spike
that ends in honest open questions has done its job. Approval is the
engineering lead's, same three fields as always.

## spec-kitti (optional path)

In repos that use spec-kitti (the client's Spec Kit fork), this epic's
technical architecture may already exist in `specs/<###-feature>/`:
`plan.md` (technical context, structure, API design, deployment),
`contracts/`, `data-model.md`. Check first, via Bash:

```bash
node .pdlc/bin/spine.js spec-kitti --ticket BANK-1234
```

If it finds the feature and `plan.md` exists, extract
`technical_architecture` and `handoff_documentation` from those files,
restated for this ticket's slice and cited in `source_docs`; the UX side
is **not** something spec-kitti produces, so draft that as usual. If it
reports no spec-kitti feature, or no `plan.md` yet, proceed unchanged.

## What to do

1. Read `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/scope.md`: `id`, `title`, `mvp_scope`,
   `success_metrics`, `impact_assessment`, `source_docs`. Confirm it is
   genuinely approved — `status: approved` **and** `approved_by` **and**
   `approval_evidence` (see "Approval" below). Approved with no evidence
   link does not clear the define gate; never proceed on a draft.
2. Decide which sections genuinely apply, per the judgment call above.
   When unsure whether an existing surface is being touched, check the
   codebase/design system rather than guessing from the ticket text.
3. Write `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/spec.md` with YAML frontmatter matching the
   `spec.v1.json` schema shipped at `.pdlc/schemas/spec.v1.json`:
   `id`/`title` from `scope.md` (`id` equal to the ticket id in
   `phase-state.json`), `cycle: 1` always (no loop-back phase here),
   `sections_included`, one-paragraph `technical_architecture` and
   `handoff_documentation` summaries with the detail in the body,
   `new_components_created` (empty array if none), `source_docs` carried
   forward, `status: draft`. Then write the body as one markdown section
   per entry in `sections_included`, using the section names as headers.
4. List anything unresolved as `open_questions` — an ambiguous technical
   decision, a component that doesn't quite fit, missing input from
   product. Do not invent answers to fill them in.
5. Stop. Do not set `status: approved` or invent an `approved_by` — that
   is the engineering lead's step, not this module's.

## Approval

When the engineering lead approves this `spec.md`, the recorded approval
needs all three of these or the design gate stays blocked:

- `status: approved`
- `approved_by: <name>`
- `approval_evidence: <link>` — the Jira comment, Slack thread, PR review, or email where the approval actually happened

`require_approval_evidence` is on for this client
(`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`), and applies even when the engineering lead
says it straight to you in the session ("looks good, approved") — a name
typed into a file is not proof. With a Jira MCP connection, use the
approval comment's permalink; without one, ask for the link.

## Example output

`pdlc/BANK-4821/spec.md`, abridged — with its full STRIDE table, OWASP
mapping and hand-off contract: `.claude/skills/spec-writer/references/worked-example.md`.

```markdown
---
id: BANK-4821
cycle: 1
title: Statement export times out for accounts with 10k+ transactions
sections_included: [technical_discovery, security_design, observability, handoff_documentation]
technical_architecture: "Export generation above 10k transactions moves to the existing background-job queue; export-api polls job status. No new services."
handoff_documentation: "export-api gains a 202 Accepted + polling response above 10k transactions, unchanged below it; no new UI states."
new_components_created: []
open_questions:
  - "[PENDING — missing: retention policy for generated export files in object storage. Information-disclosure threat has no control until this is set; needs the data-protection owner.]"
source_docs: ["https://client-docs.example.com/prd/statement-export-v2"]
status: draft
---

## Security design

Assets: statement data (financial) and the generated file at rest. STRIDE
covers spoofing on the poll endpoint, information disclosure at rest
(mitigation `[PENDING]` on the retention policy, hence the open question)
and denial of service on export creation. OWASP A01/A02/A09 map to those
controls; A03 and A10 do not apply, and the body says why.

## Observability

`export_job_duration_seconds` and `export_job_failures_total` on top of
the RED metrics already emitted; trace spans on enqueue, pickup and
object-storage write; one alert (failure rate > 2% sustained 10 minutes
→ P2) with its runbook named. No new SLO.
```

The technical-discovery and hand-off sections are cut here for length.
